# Airport CEO Brief — Quiet-by-Design as an MWAA CEO Question

*Independent Stage 1 brief. Written from the CEO's chair: bond covenants, board votes, and 30-year amortization schedules, not decibels.*

## What I am actually being asked

A "quiet-by-design standard" reads to the operator as an acoustics-and-PA specification. From my chair, it is a question about three instruments: the MWAA Airports Authority Design Manual, the 2025–2039 Use & Lease with the signatory airlines, and the political posture of the current federal-White House-USDOT-United Airlines partnership on the $20 billion–plus Dulles transformation announced on July 29, 2026 [Source: https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/americas-newest-capital-gateway-unveiled-more-than-20-billion-transformation-at-washington-dulles-international-airport-302838221.html]. The engineering is real, but the CEO decision is about where the requirement lives in our contract stack, not about which loudspeaker to buy.

If we write this into the Design Manual as a compliance envelope before Concourses C and D and the AeroTrain extension harden into procurement, the marginal cost is design fee, not capital. If we retrofit it into a program already priced into the $6.99 billion IAD CCP, into airline rates and charges, and into a residual two-airport model that already forecasts cost per enplanement rising from $12.77 to $20.71 by 2030, we have created a Majority-in-Interest problem for a $20 million–$40 million requirement [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions]. The gap between those two paths is the entire brief.

## Key findings

1. **This is a Design Manual amendment, not a capital program.** MWAA's seven-volume Airports Authority Design Manual is the natural home; the public index does not disclose explicit acoustic performance criteria (NC, RT60, STI, adjacent-zone spillover) in the current volumes [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/business/airports-authority-design-manual]. Writing them in before Concourse E's successors are designed is the cheapest lever the authority has to shape passenger-facing terminal quality across the next 15 years. Waiting until 30 percent design on a specific project turns a specification question into a change-order fight and into airline scrutiny under the Use & Lease.

2. **The bond narrative absorbs this without a wobble.** All three agencies affirmed MWAA at Aa3 / AA- / AA- stable in May 2025, and each cited the new 15-year Use & Lease and 140% coverage target as credit-supportive [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/financial-statistics/investor-relations/credit-ratings]. A design-manual change that stays inside the existing CCP envelope, strengthens the ADA/ACAA/NFPA 72 compliance posture, and reduces post-opening rework is not a rating event. It is a talking point for the next official statement.

3. **The Majority-in-Interest exposure is the actual cost, and it lives entirely in how the standard is scoped.** The public disclosures on the 2025 Use & Lease confirm the residual two-airport structure and 140% coverage target [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/news/airports-authority-board-approves-use-and-lease-agreement-airlines-addresses-reagan-dulles], but the specific MII thresholds, project categories, and any design-manual carve-outs are not in the public record. That is exactly where this decision is won or lost. Written as an owner design floor inside the Design Manual, quiet-by-design is inherited by every consultant and contractor and never triggers airline concurrence. Written as a new "PA/FIDS enhancement" capital category, it hands United (at IAD) and American (at DCA) a veto right on future passenger-experience spending.

4. **The board can pass this. It cannot pass a pilot.** The 17-member board is chaired by Mark Uncapher for 2026, with Alex Vogel as vice chair and Felice C. Smith re-elected secretary [Source: https://www.aviationpros.com/airport-business/news/55332705/metropolitan-washington-airports-authority-board-elects-2026-officers]. Members serve six-year terms with one reappointment [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/about-authority/board-directors]. Any chair sitting today will be gone before the Concourse C/D delivery cycle ends. A design-manual amendment is durable across a chair transition; a "quiet culture" is not. If we want this to survive us, it goes in writing, in the Design Manual, and in the Orders and Instructions for both airports [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/business/orders-and-instructions-iad] [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/business/orders-and-instructions-dca].

5. **The federal stack has three calendars, and the standard has to be compliant against all three without asking any of them for permission.** FAA reauthorization runs on its own cycle (the current authorization, which added the 10 beyond-perimeter DCA exemptions under §502 [Source: https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IN12504], expires in 2028); USDOT holds the ground lease and is a live political partner after the July 2026 joint announcement [Source: https://www.transportation.gov/briefing-room/prepare-takeoff-president-donald-j-trump-us-transportation-secretary-sean-p-duffy]; TSA and CBP set the queue-communication and FIS-signage floors independently. The standard has to be written as an *envelope* — minimums we exceed — not as a substitute for federal life-safety, accessibility, or security requirements. Any language that could be read as "we announce less than TSA/CBP/NFPA require" is a grant-assurance risk.

6. **IAD and DCA are one system on paper and two political properties in practice.** Uniform mandatory floors are appropriate for life-safety intelligibility (STI-PA ≥ 0.50 in evacuation zones under NFPA 72), for ADA and ACAA channel redundancy, and for employee sound exposure. Anything above the floor should vary. Saarinen's 1962 Main Terminal at IAD is a preservation-protected structural landmark [Source: https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/americas-newest-capital-gateway-unveiled-more-than-20-billion-transformation-at-washington-dulles-international-airport-302838221.html], Pelli's 1997 National Hall at DCA is a signature interior, and the 1941 Terminal 1 is a different architectural inheritance again [Source: https://www.ifly.com/airports/ronald-reagan-washington-national-airport/terminal-map]. Uniform PA density prescriptions would insult all three.

7. **The DCA operating profile sets the floor on how quiet the system can go.** DCA closed 2025 at 24.9 million passengers, down 5.3% and its lowest since 2022, after the January 2025 midair collision, the fall 2025 federal shutdown, and softer federal travel demand [Source: https://www.arlnow.com/2026/02/25/reagan-national-confirms-its-lowest-air-traffic-numbers-in-three-years/]. A single-main-runway, slot-constrained airport with a heavy federal-official passenger mix and a very short curb-to-gate journey has almost no tolerance for a missed boarding. The mandatory intelligibility floor at DCA is higher than at IAD because the operational cost of failure is higher.

8. **What we buy is optionality; what we risk is scope creep.** A written standard buys us a defensible answer to the ADA/ACAA plaintiff bar, a rating-narrative talking point, an easier 2039 Use & Lease conversation, and — if scoped correctly — a shield against ad-hoc airline demands to bolt more PA gear onto hold rooms. What we risk is White House-level attention on any element of the Dulles vision that a reporter can frame as "reducing information for the traveling public."

## The federal stack, in the currency the CEO cares about

MWAA is a public body operating under a long-term lease from USDOT [Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Washington_Airports_Authority]. That single fact means every design-manual change has to survive grant-assurance review if any element of the space it governs is touched by AIP or BIL funding. The quiet-by-design standard therefore cannot be written in a way that reduces any federally required signal — TSA screening announcements, CBP arrival information, NFPA 72 voice evacuation intelligibility, or ACAA-required airline communications. The correct framing for federal partners is *envelope above the floor*: we are specifying acoustic and information-system minimums that make the federal requirements *work better* (higher STI, less spillover, cleaner signal-to-noise), not minimums that replace them. This framing is what makes the standard survive an FAA financial compliance review and an OIG audit of AIP-funded terminal work.

The July 2026 joint announcement with the White House and USDOT [Source: https://www.transportation.gov/briefing-room/prepare-takeoff-president-donald-j-trump-us-transportation-secretary-sean-p-duffy] is a political fact I have to price into every visible decision at IAD until the current administration changes. Anything at Dulles that reads as "removing" — announcements, TVs, information — will be read politically. The name of the standard matters. "Quiet-by-design" invites the misreading; "terminal acoustic and information performance standard" does not. The internal document should carry the second name.

## Airline economics and the MII question

The 2025 Use & Lease locked a two-airport residual model, a 140% coverage target, and a $9.0 billion CCP split roughly $6.99 billion IAD / $2.39 billion DCA [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions]. United is our anchor at IAD under a long-term Dulles lease executed alongside the master agreement [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/news/airports-authority-and-united-airlines-execute-long-term-lease-dulles-international]; American is our anchor at DCA. Both are sophisticated MII counterparties.

The specific MII language in the 2025 agreement — thresholds, project categories, review triggers, and whether design-manual updates are carved out — is not in the public record for this run. That is a professional judgment call: based on how residual agreements at peer hubs are typically written, MII will attach to specific capital projects above a dollar threshold, not to design-manual amendments. That gives me a clean path: adopt the standard as a design-manual amendment by board resolution, with the marginal design cost absorbed in existing consultant scopes, and treat the incremental capital exposure as *what we would have specified anyway* if we were designing to industry current practice. The airlines cannot object to us specifying to current practice.

The one thing that would collapse this argument is if the incremental cost of quiet-by-design on any single Concourse C/D delivery package exceeds the MII trigger in that project's category. That is the diligence question I would put to General Counsel before the board memo is written. If the trigger is $50 million per project, we have room. If it is $5 million, this becomes an airline negotiation.

## The bond rating, honestly

MWAA sits at Aa3 / AA- / AA-, all stable, most recently affirmed in May 2025 [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/financial-statistics/investor-relations/credit-ratings]. The rating narrative is built on the enterprise value of the two-airport system, the strength of the new Use & Lease, and adequate coverage. The stress point in the story — and the one the agencies flagged inside the 2025A financing — is the trajectory of debt per O&D enplanement (projected to peak near $400 in 2028) and CPE (projected to rise from $12.77 to $20.71 by 2030) [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions]. Roughly $5.5 billion of new debt is planned across 2025–2028, on top of the $714.4 million Series 2025A [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions].

A design-manual amendment does not move any of those numbers. It does not add to CCP. It does not draw from PFCs, AIP, or BIL. It does not change coverage. What it does is give me one additional sentence in the "management and governance" narrative for the next official statement: MWAA has adopted a system-wide terminal acoustic and information performance standard governing new and substantially renovated terminal space. That reads well in the covered rating criteria (governance and strategy). It is a very small positive; it is not the reason we get upgraded.

The only version of this thesis that touches the rating is the version where quiet-by-design is scoped as a *separate capital program* on top of CCP — a new $200 million line item that has to be financed. That version I would not bring to the board, because the marginal spread cost on $200 million of debt at our current rating (call it 15–25 basis points of narrative erosion if the story got sloppy on top of a rising CPE curve) is worth more than the standard buys us. The correct scoping is envelope, not program.

## The financial-and-political case, as the CEO reads it

**For.** The current CCP is where terminal specifications for the next two decades get locked in. The Concourse C/D replacement, the AeroTrain extension, and the retirement of the mobile lounges will produce more than 5 million square feet of new or renovated terminal space at IAD [Source: https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/americas-newest-capital-gateway-unveiled-more-than-20-billion-transformation-at-washington-dulles-international-airport-302838221.html]. If we do not write acoustic and information-system requirements into the Design Manual now, we will inherit whatever the individual A/E teams specify project by project — inconsistent between concourses at the same airport, inconsistent between IAD and DCA, and inconsistent with peer-hub current practice. That is the failure mode a CEO who has watched a large program through delivery knows to avoid. The best time to write the standard was in 2023. The second-best time is before the C/D program brief is issued.

**Against.** The thesis solves a problem the board has not asked me to solve, at a moment when board and White House attention on the Dulles program is at a maximum. It creates a headline risk (any "quiet" framing is easily misread) and a professional-services cost (specialist acoustic consultants, PA/FIDS zoning studies, commissioning agents) that has to be defended in front of airline signatories already looking at a CPE curve moving from $12.77 to $20.71. If we scope it wrong and pull an MII trigger, we open a negotiation we do not need to open until 2039. And if the current federal partners read a change to the Dulles passenger environment as inconsistent with their July 2026 vision, we spend political capital on a design detail.

**How I would decide.** Approve as a Design Manual amendment, not as a capital program. Name it accurately (terminal acoustic and information performance standard). Scope it as a compliance envelope above the federal floor, not a substitute for any federally required signal. Require the standard to be met by consultant teams inside existing design fees. Reserve the mandatory tier for life-safety intelligibility, ADA/ACAA channel redundancy, and employee exposure, and let terminal-specific implementation flex above that floor. Bring the memo to the board as a governance strengthening item paired with the next CCP progress report, not as a standalone. Do not use the word "quiet" in the resolution.

## Data points and precedents a strategist can use

1. **MWAA credit posture:** Moody's Aa3, S&P AA-, Fitch AA-, all stable, affirmed May 2025 [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/financial-statistics/investor-relations/credit-ratings]. The rating narrative for the next official statement is the leverage point where a governance strengthening reads well.

2. **The airline economics we are already asking the signatories to absorb:** CPE $12.77 today rising to a forecast $20.71 by 2030, adjusted debt per O&D enplanement peaking near $400 in 2028, roughly $5.5 billion new debt across 2025–2028 [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions]. This is the pressure background against which any incremental request has to be sold. Any recommendation with a capital ask attached is a hard sell; any recommendation that stays inside existing scope is not.

3. **The CCP scope where the standard would land:** $9.0 billion total, ~$6.99 billion IAD / ~$2.39 billion DCA under the 2025 Use & Lease [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions], with more than 5 million square feet of new or renovated IAD terminal space in the pipeline [Source: https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/americas-newest-capital-gateway-unveiled-more-than-20-billion-transformation-at-washington-dulles-international-airport-302838221.html]. That square-footage is what the standard is protecting from inconsistency.

4. **The board and chair-transition arithmetic:** 17 seats, six-year terms with one reappointment, four appointing authorities [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/about-authority/board-directors]. A design-manual amendment survives chair transitions; an unwritten culture does not.

5. **The DCA operating floor:** 24.9 million passengers in 2025, down 5.3% year-over-year, on a single-main-runway airport with slot-constrained operations and heavy federal travel exposure [Source: https://www.arlnow.com/2026/02/25/reagan-national-confirms-its-lowest-air-traffic-numbers-in-three-years/]. The mandatory intelligibility floor at DCA is higher than at IAD because the operational cost of a missed boarding is higher and because the January 2025 midair collision is still in the public memory.

## What this brief is not saying

I am not saying quiet-by-design is a bad idea. I am saying the version that succeeds is a Design Manual amendment written before the C/D program brief is issued, scoped as an envelope above federal floors, named accurately, and priced inside existing design fees. That version is a cheap, durable, board-survivable governance improvement that reads well in the next rating cycle. Every other version — pilot programs, retrofit initiatives, standalone capital lines, "quiet airport" branding — is a distraction from the CCP delivery cycle we are already carrying, and I would not spend the political capital.

## Evidence gaps I did not fill

- Whether the current Airports Authority Design Manual already contains acoustic performance criteria in a volume the public index does not surface. This is a diligence item, not a research item.
- The specific MII thresholds, categories, and design-manual carve-outs in the 2025 Use & Lease. The public press release confirms the residual two-airport structure and the 140% coverage target but not the MII mechanics. This determines whether the recommendation is a board memo or an airline negotiation.
- Financing structure for the incremental $22.5 billion Dulles vision beyond the $7 billion IAD CCP [Source: https://www.ffxnow.com/2026/07/31/dulles-partners-mostly-mum-on-details-of-how-22-5b-expansion-plan-will-be-funded/]. Until that structure is clear, the political ceiling on any Dulles-related decision is uncertain.
